Ascension Simulator 2015

So, it all begins with this shipwreck. You take the role of this indigent person, with no past or memory to provide a small amount of immersion. You can't edit their name or appearance, so it's like: "Here's what we've got available. You can either like it or suck it".
As for the story, it's just another of those games where you're supposedly a chosen hero who can save the realm, so there's not really much to expect. The graphics are really cool, I don't really have nothing against them. As for the sound effects and soundtrack, they're ok too. The voice acting is so bad and cheesy that it made me laugh at certain parts. The NPC characters don't have much charisma, except maybe for Fred, the skeleton. Now, when it comes to the gameplay, this game gets really bizarre. The controls are really weird, it takes quite a while to get used to them, and even if you're really far in the game, they can still give you a lot of headache. The jumping system is pretty messed up too: When you're going up, you look like a freaking rag doll. While on the regular ground, it makes your character look like it's on the moon; on the bright side, it's also a really handy way to travel faster across the map.
The level up system in this game is really unique. By that, I mean that whenever you reach a specific amount of points, you can't simply level up. You've gotta find a certain kind of trainer that can help you learn the skills that you want, but adding insult to injury, you still gotta pay them a load of money. That by itself is ridiculous, but the worst part is that the loot that you usually find in this game is so cheap, that you can hardly buy a lollipop. So, what are the alternatives? Well, since it's an open-world kind of game, you can get to commit certain crimes. So the game basically forces you to become a scoundrel, so you can get a chance to advance. Well, since we're at it, if you're not strong enough to deal with a certain enemy, you can get to kite them closer to someone who's stronger than you, let them fight to the death, and do your best to deliver the final blow, otherwise you won't get the experience points.
Another really stupid thing about this game, is that you can't get to buy armor in regular vendors. Here's how it works: There're two different factions in the game. You can get to choose one, and when you worked enough for them, you can receive a promotion. Along with it, you can get to get a new armor, but again, you still gotta pay for the damn thing. So it's like your boss decide to make you the new sales manager, but in order to do that, you still gotta pay for the room and all the furniture inside.
Well, can this game get more bizarre than that? It actually can. Even though the map isn't ginormous, the navigation tools really suck, you gotta keep accessing the inventory to see where you're heading to. The fast travel system is present, but you've gotta find runes of the locations in order to unlock them. As for the inventory itself, that's actually something positive, as you can get to store the whole universe inside of your pocket.
Another thing that I find necessary to mention, is that you're gonna surely bump into one or more glitches throughout the game. Here're some that I've found during my gameplay:
- Sometimes your character may get stuck on a rock, trying to regain his balance for an endless amount of time. Whenever that happens, your only option available is to reload your last save;
- When you're inside of caves and trying to climb a wall, your character might fly to the top of the screen and simply disappear. You can't even access the menu whenever that happens, so say hello to CTRL+ALT+DEL (lots of times);
- I was entering the blacksmith shop once, and all of a sudden I've appeared at the top of the roof;
- Whenever you find an item, you need to click on it in order to get it (yeah, that's really annoying). For some reason, I've managed to pick something without having to click on it;
- If there's a certain item that it's really high and you can't reach, try to look from different angles. I've managed to pick up some stuff using this method;
- I was really shocked when I've seen this farmer walking across the streets without actually moving his body;
- Invisible platforms: If you're really impatient to get down a mountain, all you've gotta do is to attempt a leap of faith. Your character will roll over some invisible platforms until you get to the floor.
So, what is there to say about these glitches? They were really funny in the beginning, but it didn't take long for me to actually lose my patience. I've searched for fixes and patches, but I couldn't find any available, so it's like: "Good freaking luck, sucker". During the load screens, the game advises you to save your game often. I think that it would be more useful if they'd inform the players that whenever their character disappear through the roof of a cavern, that they shouldn't forget to press CTRL+ALT+DEL.
Just to finish, I don't even know why I'm recommending this game. I guess I just love to have a crappy experience while I'm playing games. Ah, and before I forget, if you decide to buy this game and decide to actually play it, whenever you're in doubt, try "Nautilus transformation". It usually works things out.

